$ ansible-doc ping
> MODULE [1mansible.builtin.ping[0m (ping.py)

  A trivial test module, this module always returns `pong' on
  successful contact. It does not make sense in playbooks, but it is
  useful from `/usr/bin/ansible' to verify the ability to login and
  that a usable Python is configured.
  This is NOT ICMP ping, this is just a trivial test module that
  requires Python on the remote-node.
  For Windows targets, use the ansible.windows.win_ping module
  instead.
  For Network targets, use the ansible.netcommon.net_ping module
  instead.

[1mOPTIONS[0m (red indicates it is required):

   data    Data to return for the `ping' return value.
           If this parameter is set to `crash', the module will cause
           an exception.
        default: pong
        type: str

[1mATTRIBUTES:[0m

        [4mcheck_mode:[0m
        description: Can run in check_mode and return changed status prediction without modifying
          target, if not supported the action will be skipped.
        support: full

        [4mdiff_mode:[0m
        description: Will return details on what has changed (or possibly needs changing in
          check_mode), when in diff mode
        support: none

        [4mplatform:[0m
        description: Target OS/families that can be operated against
        platforms: posix
        support: N/A

[1mSEE ALSO:[0m
      * Module ansible.netcommon.net_ping
      * Module ansible.windows.win_ping

[1mAUTHOR[0m: Ansible Core Team, Michael DeHaan

[1mEXAMPLES:[0m
# Test we can logon to 'webservers' and execute python with json lib.
# ansible webservers -m ansible.builtin.ping

- name: Example from an Ansible Playbook
  ansible.builtin.ping:

- name: Induce an exception to see what happens
  ansible.builtin.ping:
    data: crash

[1mRETURN VALUES:[0m

   ping    Value provided with the `data' parameter.
        returned: success
        sample: pong
        type: str

